diff --git a/docs/browser-freshness.md b/docs/browser-freshness.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..cc7a94b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/browser-freshness.md
@@ -0,0 +1,76 @@
+# Browser snapshot freshness
+
+The dashboard replaces its entire displayed node snapshot on each successful
+response. Removed nodes and map points disappear; summaries are calculated from
+that response. An empty snapshot shows **No nodes reported** and zero connected
+nodes. A missing value is shown as unavailable rather than an invented zero;
+actual zero ticket/pending values remain zero. The old hardcoded 12.99-second
+average is removed; average block time remains unavailable.
+
+The highest reported block summary uses currently connected nodes. The ticket,
+pending and block-received summaries refer to that selected node. Ties use the
+node ID for deterministic ordering. This is a report comparison, not a canonical
+chain selector. Version strings no longer determine the highest block, and old
+high-water values do not survive a replacement snapshot. Full block hashes remain
+in the parsed report and are shortened only for display.
+
+## Required browser configuration
+
+Set these public values at build time:
+
+- `REACT_APP_STATS_API_PATH`: the same-origin API prefix, such as `/stats-api`.
+- `REACT_APP_STATS_POLL_INTERVAL_MS`: delay after a completed or failed request.
+- `REACT_APP_STATS_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS`: request deadline before cancellation.
+
+Both durations are required positive integers at most 2147483647. There are no
+production defaults. Select them with the ingestion interval and API age budget:
+the next poll should normally complete before the displayed snapshot expires.
+Tests use 100 ms and 50 ms respectively; those are test values, not launch advice.
+Configuration validation runs when the browser module loads.
+
+## Failure and expiry behavior
+
+The API supplies the observation timestamp in `X-Dashboard-Observed-At` and the
+remaining freshness budget in `X-Dashboard-Valid-For-Ms`. Keep both headers through
+the same-origin proxy, along with `Cache-Control: no-store`; do not cache these
+responses. API and browser must be deployed together for this header contract.
+
+The browser subtracts the elapsed request time, measured with `performance.now`,
+from that budget. This deliberately expires conservatively and does not compare
+the server clock to the user's wall clock. The browser checks response metadata,
+row timestamps, distinct IDs and the report fields it renders. A response already
+expired in transit is unavailable. The expiry timer runs independently of polling
+and clears the current rows, map and summary values even if another request hangs.
+
+HTTP errors, invalid responses and request timeouts also clear current values.
+The status message says data is unavailable or expired and that retries continue.
+The last successfully displayed observation time remains explicitly labeled
+**Last snapshot**. Error details and response payloads are not logged to the UI.
+
+Only one request is pending. The deadline cancels it; late callbacks cannot replace
+a newer view. A completed or failed request schedules one next poll. Unmounting
+cancels the request and timers. Returning to a visible tab clears its view and
+requests a new snapshot because browsers may throttle timers while hidden.
+JavaScript suspension can delay any UI update; the visibility refresh is not a
+guarantee that a suspended process executes an expiry callback on time.
+
+The **Connected** label describes the collector's cached connection status.
+Neither that status nor a fresh stored snapshot proves recent reports from each
+node, chain agreement, correct ticket funding, or working auto-buy. Per-node
+report timestamps and comparison with real efsn/RPC remain separate requirements.
+
+## Local checks and remaining build blocker
+
+Root `npm test` includes the polling/projection contracts and API header checks.
+In `react-frontend`, set the three settings above (the DOM tests expect `/stats-api`,
+100 and 50), then run `CI=true npm test -- --watchAll=false --runInBand` using your
+shell's environment syntax. These tests render the actual React component in
+JSDOM with a controlled HTTP adapter and clock. They are not a deployed browser,
+actual node, PostgreSQL or TLS test.
+
+The locked frontend dependencies install with scripts disabled and no lock change.
+On Node 22.11.0 the inherited `react-scripts` 3.2.0 production build fails in
+webpack's hashing path with `ERR_OSSL_EVP_UNSUPPORTED`, both before and after this
+change. No legacy crypto switch, edited dependency file, runtime downgrade or
+dependency upgrade is included. A reviewed build-tool/runtime correction and
+then actual browser/proxy acceptance are required before release.
